--TEST--
A failed assertion throws `Core\Test\Failure`, which is an ordinary `Throwable` a program catches, constructs and re-raises -- `rule:testing/failure-ledger`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's whole first half: catching one is "not only allowed, it is necessary",
// so this case runs each of the three shapes that need to -- a composite
// assertion that re-raises, a test *of* an assertion, and the plain root catch
// a ported program writes -- and counts their agreements rather than reading
// the class off one line.

use Core\Test;

// The failing subjects. Each member of § 4's roster funnels through one throw
// site, so the class is asked of all three rather than of the first.
final class Subject {
    public static function same(): void { Test::assertSame(1, 2); }
    public static function equals(): void { Test::assertEquals("a", "b"); }
    public static function deep(): void {
        array<int> $actual = [1, 2];
        array<int> $expected = [1, 3];
        Test::assertEqualsDeep($actual, $expected);
    }
}

int $agreed = 0;

// 1. The name catches it, and the root catches the same throw -- one class in
// the tree, reached by either spelling.
try {
    Subject::same();
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $named) {
    echo "by name: ", $named->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Subject::same();
} catch (Throwable $root) {
    echo "by root: ", $root->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// 2. Every member of the roster lands in the same class.
try {
    Subject::equals();
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromEquals) {
    echo "equals: ", $fromEquals->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

try {
    Subject::deep();
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromDeep) {
    echo "deep: ", $fromDeep->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// 3. It is an ordinary `Throwable` in the class graph, not a class of its own
// kind -- which is what makes the root catch above the same catch.
try {
    Subject::same();
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $probe) {
    if ($probe is Throwable) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}

// 4. § 5's worked example: a user-written composite assertion intercepts one
// and raises its own, with the inherited constructor spec § 10 gives the root.
final class UserCheck {
    public static function assertValid(int $n): void {
        try {
            Test::assertSame($n, 7);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $inner) {
            throw new Core\Test\Failure("user invalid: " . $inner->message);
        }
    }
}

try {
    UserCheck::assertValid(3);
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $outer) {
    echo "composite: ", $outer->message, "\n";
    $agreed = $agreed + 1;
}

// A passing assertion raises nothing at all, which is the half that says the
// throw is the failure and not the call.
Test::assertSame(7, 7);
UserCheck::assertValid(7);
echo "a passing assertion throws nothing\n";

echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/5\n";
--EXPECT--
by name: Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 1, `$expected` is 2
by root: Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 1, `$expected` is 2
equals: Core\Test::assertEquals failed: `$actual` is "a", `$expected` is "b"
deep: Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep failed: the two differ at `$actual["1"]`: `$actual` is 2, `$expected` is 3
composite: user invalid: Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 3, `$expected` is 7
a passing assertion throws nothing
agreed=5/5
